+# The main theorems with two hypotheses
+
+`RamareMoebius` is a consequence of `PlattGRH`. H. A. Helfgott's RSBounds project proves
+
+```lean
+theorem TernaryGoldbach.RSBounds.Moebius003.ramareMoebius_of_plattGRH
+    (h : TernaryGoldbach.PlattGRH) : TernaryGoldbach.RamareMoebius
+```
+
+from this repository's `Explicit.rh_upto_1e8` (RH up to `10⁸` from `PlattGRH` at `q = 1`) and a
+kernel-checked proof of `|∑_{n≤x} μ(n)/n| ≤ 0.03/log x` for `x ≥ 11815` from RH up to
+`1894438.5122`. This package uses it to state the main theorems without `hμ`:
+
+```lean
+theorem TernaryGoldbach.ternary_goldbach_of_two_hypotheses (hGRH : PlattGRH)
+    (hBG : BinaryGoldbachInput) : Statement
+theorem TernaryGoldbach.ternary_goldbach_large_of_plattGRH (hGRH : PlattGRH) :
+    TernaryGoldbachFrom (10 ^ 27)
+theorem TernaryGoldbach.ramareMoebius_of_plattGRH (hGRH : PlattGRH) : RamareMoebius
+```
+
+## Why a separate package
+
+RSBounds requires this repository, so the root package cannot require RSBounds without a
+dependency cycle. Copying RSBounds in would add about 2,400 modules and 40 MB of source, most of
+it generated certificates, and about ten hours of build. Instead this directory is its own Lake
+package. It requires the root package from `..` and RSBounds from git. The root package does not
+know about it: `lake build` and `comparator/config.json` at the root are unchanged, and the
+three-hypothesis theorems stay as they are.
+
+`lakefile.lean` requires `TernaryGoldbach` before RSBounds. Lake resolves a package name to the
+root's own requirement, so RSBounds is compiled against this checkout, not against the commit it
+pins. If a change here breaks RSBounds, this package stops building and the root does not.
+
+## Checking it
+
+From this directory:
+
+```sh
+lake update
+lake exe cache get
+lake build TwoHypotheses TwoHypothesesChallenge
+lake comparator --config comparator.json
+```
+
+`lake build` builds all of the root package's development that `MainTheorems` needs and RSBounds's
+proof of `RamareMoebius`. Comparator checks the three theorems against `TwoHypothesesChallenge.lean`,
+whose definitions are those of `../Challenge.lean` unchanged, and permits only `propext`,
+`Quot.sound` and `Classical.choice`. Success ends with
+
+```
+Lean default kernel accepts the solution
+Your solution is okay!
+```
